    <h3 style="color: #ffffff; font-size: 1.1rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 10px; line-height: 1.4;">Signed Condition Sign‑off</h3>
    <p style="color: #94a3b8; font-size: 0.88rem; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0;">After the visual and mileage checks, the recipient reviews the inspection log, confirms agreement with the findings, and signs the handover form. The signature serves as legal acknowledgment that the vehicle was delivered in the documented state, completing the transfer of responsibility.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Both parties must retain copies of the signed handover form, the inspection log, and the LR bilty for future reference. Any discrepancies identified during the daylight re‑inspection should be photographed, annotated, and reported to the logistics coordinator within 24 hours to initiate a claim process.</p>

<p>Best practices include using a standardized checklist, ensuring the inspection area is free from shadows, and employing calibrated odometer reading tools. Training delivery personnel on these protocols reduces the likelihood of errors and fosters trust between the shipper and the recipient.</p>

<p>By rigorously following these three steps—daylight condition re‑inspection, odometer verification, and signed condition sign‑off—the handover process becomes a reliable, auditable event that safeguards the interests of all stakeholders involved in two‑wheeler and motorcycle deliveries.</p></div>
